2. Shared engineering concept WITHOUT shared runtime dependency
The programs may have similar architectural patterns, but their distributed folders must be self-contained and independent. Device communication and configuration logic must be separate from presentation logic. Each application owns a server-side task manager; no SSH or serial transport belongs to a web-page object or per-browser-request lifecycle. Tasks are given immutable IDs and store progress/results in backend memory plus local durable records (SQLite/JSON as appropriate). On page refresh or browser reconnect, reattach to an existing task ID and display current progress without restarting the device operation. Closing or navigating away from a tab must not automatically drop a running session; terminating the Python process will terminate the sessions, and the GUI must state this clearly.

Session reliability requirements:
- Configurable connection timeout, authentication timeout, command timeout, and idle timeout / keepalive only when appropriate.
- A clear connection owner, connection/serial locks, safe cleanup, resource release, and no two tasks using the same physical serial session concurrently.
- Direct SSH can use bounded concurrency across independent targets; a single serial console → SSH Jump is sequential, never concurrent over one console.
- Verify device identity and trust SSH host keys according to approved enrollment. Never blindly accept or disable verification; gateway-to-target trust is distinct from laptop-to-device trust.
- Correct handling of password, privilege/enable prompts, paging, host-key prompts, remote exit and gateway-prompt recovery. If a jump session cannot safely return to the gateway context, STOP the remaining jump batch (do not guess device identity).
- Every config-changing action records explicit target, interface, attempted command outcome, read-back outcome, and unresolved/ambiguous outcome when connection dies after sending commands. No automatic blind retries of uncertain writes.
- Runtime credentials never stored in plaintext databases/logs, never printed on the browser, never shipped in examples. No task starts on application launch.
- Allow graceful Stop/Cancel, but explain that Cancel is not a rollback of an already-sent command.
- Fail-skip independent targets where safe; do not continue when shared console context is ambiguous.
- Default to simulated device mode; switching to live mode must require an explicit user selection and confirmation, and real devices are reserved for authorized post-development lab tests.
3. Program 1 — Interface & Tunnel Manager
Purpose: enable an engineer/vendor to check a chosen interface's operational and administrative status and apply shutdown / no shutdown across multiple selected routers. Primary disaster-recovery use case: HQ's primary overlay tunnels fail, a technician consoles into a branch router, then SSHes from that gateway to other branches over independently reachable WAN/MPLS IP addresses to open backup tunnel interfaces. HQ services, HQ loopbacks, HQ VPN, or centralized AAA must not be mandatory dependencies; allow an authorized local fallback login when appropriate.

Connection modes:
1. Direct SSH: select multiple routers and use each router's management/reachable IP; bounded parallel SSH and independent per-target status/error.
2. Direct Console: enumerate and select COM port, configurable baud (default 9600), handle serial login/privilege and selected local router.
3. Console → SSH Jump: choose local console gateway and multiple remote target routers; use the targets' WAN IP; gateway and target runtime credentials can differ; include the gateway itself as a target if selected; process all target SSH sessions sequentially through the ONE console, recovering verified gateway prompt between targets; show each target result.
Device inventory locally managed through GUI, with Add/Edit/Delete, multi-select, search, import/export CSV/XLSX and per-device hostname, management IP, WAN IP, vendor/platform/model, site and notes. Do not preload any real corporate data. Match target hostname/prompt identity before changes.

Interface selection:
- Same Interface on all selected routers (e.g. Tunnel90).
- Per-Device Interface selection (e.g. Tunnel10, Tunnel90, Tunnel500, GigabitEthernet0/0/0), with support for flexible names and optionally multiple interface names per device.
- Vendor interface selection remains FLEXIBLE; do not limit it to one predefined tunnel. Device account privileges remain the true authorization control.
Exactly three prominent actions: Check Status, No Shutdown, Shutdown. Do not add a separate multi-step pre-check wizard or require Check Status before changes. For write actions, require an explicit confirmation listing devices/interfaces. After executing, perform a read-back status check. Provide statuses UP (up/up), DOWN (down/down), ADMIN DOWN, UP/DOWN, NOT FOUND, FAILED, UNKNOWN and distinguish command accepted from interface actually up. Never invent status from command response alone.

Results table columns: device, target IP, connection mode, interface, admin/operational state, action, action result, timestamp and explanation. Live operation logs, execution progress, count success/failed/skipped, history, copy/export results, clear logs, cancellable queued tasks. Make meaningful error handling (login, timeout, unknown interface, unsupported platform, lost serial context).

Implement real adapters where support and syntax are verified, alongside tested simulated/mock transports. In particular, support Cisco IOS/IOS-XE router use cases under documented platform assumptions; do not claim Huawei router support without adapter coverage.
4. Program 2 — 802.1X Recovery Manager (Internal IT only)
Purpose: temporarily recover selected switch access ports when endpoint 802.1X authentication fails. A static data VLAN is applied during recovery instead of depending on a RADIUS dynamic VLAN; preserve any existing static voice VLAN, QoS, STP and other critical settings, and restore the exact relevant original state later. This is a separate IT-only application—never shipped alongside the vendor program.

Supported target design: Cisco Catalyst with 802.1X/MAB and Huawei S5735/S5700 PoE with hybrid ports and authentication profiles. Use separate version-aware vendor adapters and safe refusal on unsupported firmware, port types, ambiguous policies or syntax; do not guess device configuration commands.

Local workbook import from Vlan_User.xlsx (not committed):
- Sum_Vlan (also tolerate reasonable capitalization/spacing variants): pattern-based reference data VLAN mapping by switch name, floor and side.
- Sum_switch: initial switch inventory, editable via GUI.
- Special user / Specail user: columns SW Name and Interface; these exact switch+port matches receive recovery VLAN 27.
- Normalize whitespace, headers, interface aliases and switch-name matching; prevent accidental collisions in similar patterns (e.g., ASW_9_1 vs ASW_9_2); flag missing/ambiguous mapping instead of guessing.
VLAN assignment priority, exactly: (1) Manual per-interface override, (2) Special-user match → VLAN 27, (3) reference VLAN pattern. There is NO switch-wide override mode. Display each selected port's chosen data VLAN and its source before changes; allow specific port exceptions. Never automatically replace original voice VLAN with data VLAN mapping.

Recovery process:
1. Select one/many switches and specific interface(s), or deliberately choose bulk access-port recovery.
2. Read device model/OS, interface type, current configuration and original static voice VLAN; for Cisco, note that RADIUS-derived dynamic data VLAN may NOT be in running config.
3. Save durable original per-device/per-port configuration snapshot first, with timestamp, device identity, session ID and checksums; a failed snapshot blocks change.
4. Resolve recovery data VLAN and its source; check local VLAN existence/port eligibility; exclude trunk, uplink, management, port-channel and unknown/special-purpose ports by default.
5. Create and show before/after diff including authentication, VLAN, voice, QoS, STP, LLDP, service-policy, description and other relevant interface config. Require explicit review/confirmation.
6. Apply selective authentication changes preferentially, preserve unrelated settings and assign the mapped static data VLAN. Be precise about Cisco 802.1X vs MAB and Huawei interface-bound authentication profiles and PVID/untagged/tagged hybrid VLAN behavior; do not remove shared/global auth profiles or break voice VLAN.
7. Verify post-change configuration and actual state, recording success, failure and ambiguities per port; fail-skip other independent eligible ports where safe.
8. Advanced default interface / reset-and-reapply is allowed ONLY with a complete device-specific validated template, a verified backup and a special warning; never perform a blind reset or assume data/voice VLAN are the only original configuration.
Restore process:
- Select a prior recovery session and interfaces; compare current config to the saved original and the recovery plan, flag conflicts/unknown out-of-band changes instead of overwriting silently.
- Remove temporary static VLAN and temporary authentication changes, restore original relevant commands and VLAN membership in an adapter-specific order, verify readback and log individual port outcome.
- For originally RADIUS-assigned data VLANs, do not persist a static data VLAN as the 'restored dynamic VLAN'; restore the 802.1X/MAB behavior so RADIUS can assign dynamically after successful authentication.
- Include recovery/restore history and exportable redacted logs. Never assume success without readback.
